    "created_at": "2017-10-31T22:44:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-10-31T22:44:09Z",
    "body": "OK, I see:\r\n\r\n> The canvas element has two attributes to control the size of the element's bitmap: width and height.\r\n\r\nand\r\n\r\n> The intrinsic dimensions of the canvas element when it represents embedded content are equal to the dimensions of the element's bitmap.\r\n\r\nI suggest another sentence saying that the width and height do actually change automatically if the underlying bitmap changes. I think bitmaprenderer is the only instance where this can happen without explicitly going through width and height.\r\n\r\nIn fact, I'm not even sure this is true. WebGL is allowed to ignore the values of width and height if that would allocate too large a backing store. It exposes drawingBufferWidth/Height in that case, while leaving the canvas width and height alone.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
